## Explicit Copying with .clone()

Since assignment MOVES, how do you get two independent copies? Use `.clone()`:

```xiom
fn main() -> Int {
  let a = 5;
  let b = a.clone();  // Deep copy — both a and b are valid
  io.println(a);       // Prints 5
  io.println(b);       // Prints 5
  return a + b;
}
```

### Clone Creates Independent Values

```xiom
fn main() -> Int {
  let v1 = Vec[Int].new();
  v1.push(1);
  v1.push(2);

  let v2 = v1.clone();  // Deep copy of entire Vec
  v1.push(3);            // Only affects v1
  v2.push(4);            // Only affects v2

  io.println(v1.len());  // 3
  io.println(v2.len());  // 3
  return v1.len() as Int;
}
```

### Clone is Explicit — No Surprises

```xiom
let data = load_large_dataset();
let backup = data.clone();  // You KNOW this is expensive — it says "clone"!
```

### When to Clone

- When you need two independent owners of the same data
- When you want to keep the original after passing to a function
- When the compiler tells you a value was moved and you still need it

Clone is your deliberate escape hatch from move semantics.

